          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security & Cost of Living", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $63,640 median household income with 74% homeownership and a 26% renter rate, FL-HD-10 is a working-class district where property insurance costs, property tax fairness, housing affordability, and whether wage growth is keeping pace with inflation are the material concerns that drove the 74-26 Republican result. Democratic challengers will target this constituency on housing costs and whether the state government is prioritizing working families' financial security. A representative who delivers concrete legislative answers on insurance regulation, property tax relief, and economic growth that translates to wage gains can hold this margin; one who treats it as inherited loyalty risks losing it." },
          { name: "Below-Average College Education & Working-Class Problem-Solving", tag: "lean-into", why: "College attainment at 20.3% is significantly below state and national norms, indicating a constituency that does not default to respecting educational credentials and instead evaluates on direct results and practical problem-solving. This is a working-class district where candidates succeed by demonstrating they have fixed specific problems — infrastructure, job creation, cost control — not by citing educational pedigree or abstract policy frameworks. Messaging and constituent service must foreground practical results over credential-based authority." },
          { name: "Undefined 4.5% Demographic Gap & Potential Hispanic/Immigrant Population", tag: "careful", why: "The gap between reported categories (95.57%) and 100% leaves 4.5% unaccounted for — a population that could include working-class Hispanic voters, immigrant communities, or other groups whose economic concerns and candidate preferences are not yet clarified by available data. In Florida's current political context, working-class Hispanic voters have shifted rightward but remain persuadable on cost-of-living and economic opportunity concerns. Understanding this 4.5% population's specific needs and ensuring they receive direct constituent service is essential to maintaining the current margin against challenger mobilization." },
          { name: "Treating 74-26 as a Durable Mandate Rather Than a Working-Class Margin Requiring Active Defense", tag: "avoid", why: "A twenty-point margin is strong on its face, but a working-class district at $63,640 income is economically vulnerable to inflation, cost-of-living shocks, and housing-market dynamics that can shift working-family economic evaluations rapidly. At 40.2 median age without college education as the norm, this is a constituency that votes on whether government is delivering for them right now, not on abstract loyalty to the Republican brand. Complacency about the 74-26 result — assuming it reflects a stable base rather than a coalition that must be actively maintained — is the primary strategic risk to holding the seat." },
          { name: "Renter Households Becoming Invisible in a Homeowner-Majority Strategy", tag: "avoid", why: "At 26% renter rate, approximately one-quarter of the district is renters facing different economic pressures than homeowners — rental affordability, tenant protections, and whether economic growth translates to wage gains for non-property-owning households are their direct concerns. A representative who focuses constituency service exclusively on homeowner priorities (property taxes, insurance, appreciation) while neglecting renter households' cost-of-living pressure invites Democratic mobilization around economic anxiety among a meaningful minority constituency. Renter households in working-class districts are electorally volatile; ignoring them is strategically dangerous." }
