          { name: "College-Educated Suburban Voter Consolidation", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 30.2% college education and an affluent suburban demographic profile, FL-HD-11 includes college-educated voters who have proven electorally responsive to competence-based messaging and evidence of effective governance in other Florida suburban districts. The 69% Republican margin suggests current consolidation of this group, but the absence of polarization rhetoric or identity-driven messaging creates an opening for a challenger who frames an election around governance competence and specific failures in constituent service rather than party label." },
          { name: "Renter Community & Housing Affordability Pressure", tag: "careful", why: "The 24.1% renter rate in an otherwise homeowner-dominant district (75.9%) represents approximately one-quarter of the electorate with materially different economic interests than property owners: younger families and professionals facing housing cost constraints, turnover-driven instability, and potentially different priorities around schools, transit, and affordable housing availability. If renter demographic churn accelerates or renters become mobilized around housing affordability concerns, the incumbent's property-owner-focused messaging may fail to consolidate this growing subset, creating vulnerability among a constituency with lower inherited partisan loyalty." },
          { name: "Assuming 69% Reflects an Unearned Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "The 69% result reflects accurate representation of current district demographics and existing voter preference alignment — not an electoral cushion that permits neglect of constituent service or shift toward partisan ideology. Affluent suburban districts maintain strong Republican margins only through continuous visible delivery on material quality-of-life expectations; any incumbent who treats 69% as permission to reduce constituent attention or shift toward abstract messaging will find a credible challenger successfully reframing the election around specific governance failures." },
          { name: "Ignoring Black and Hispanic Community Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "The combined 12.28% Black and 11.97% Hispanic populations represent roughly 24% of the district's electorate, yet the 69-31 result suggests minimal Democratic performance among these communities — indicating either low mobilization or meaningful crossover Republican voting. Any incumbent who assumes continued Republican performance among these communities without active constituent engagement risks a challenger who invests specifically in voter contact, persuasion, and turnout infrastructure around economic mobility, healthcare access, and education quality concerns that affect minority families in affluent suburbs at higher rates than white homeowners." }
