          { name: "Asian Community Engagement & Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 5.65% Asian population represents approximately 1,800-2,000 residents in a typical Florida House district — large enough to meaningfully influence a competitive election — and has demonstrated in neighboring Florida districts to evaluate on business opportunity, immigration policy clarity, and educational access rather than default partisan alignment. This community is currently contained within the 57% Republican result but is not deeply polarized; a challenger who invests in targeted outreach on immigrant business support, H-1B clarity, and educational funding can shift this constituency's preference. Proactive engagement with Asian-owned business networks, community organizations, and educational institutions prevents future Democratic gains in what is currently a persuadable demographic." },
          { name: "Renter & Younger Adult Economic Concerns", tag: "careful", why: "The 29.6% renter rate in a district with $68,484 median income and 36-year median age indicates a meaningful population of younger renters and service-sector workers who may not own the assets that anchor the 70.6% homeowner base to Republican economic positioning. While currently contained within the 43% Democratic opposition, this constituency's economic concerns around housing affordability, wage growth, and healthcare access could mobilize higher turnout if a credible challenger makes a targeted appeal. Monitoring younger renter sentiment and addressing housing cost concerns proactively prevents this demographic from becoming a concentrated source of future erosion." },
          { name: "Treating the 57% Result as a Durable Majority Without Active Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "A 57% result in a suburban district with above-average education, above-average income, and meaningful minority populations is not inherently durable without sustained constituent engagement. The college-educated plurality and Asian community are both persuadable demographics that do not default to Republican alignment; assuming this vote share will hold without active outreach to these constituencies is a strategic error that has cost Republican incumbents in similar suburban Florida markets." },
          { name: "Neglecting the 43% Opposition as a Homogeneous Democratic Base", tag: "avoid", why: "The assumption that 43% opposition is entirely composed of ideological Democrats who will not be persuaded is incorrect; in a suburban, college-educated, economically stable district with a meaningful Asian population, opposition voters are more likely to be persuadable on specific issues than uniformly partisan. Campaigning as though the opposition is monolithic rather than investigating whether opposition is concentrated among younger renters, among college-educated voters frustrated with specific Republican positions, or among the Asian community leaves gaps that a credible challenger can exploit." }
