          { name: "Renter and Younger Adult Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 25.5% renter rate and median age of 52.5 together signal a constituency with a substantial younger, working-age minority whose economic concerns differ sharply from the older homeowner majority. Housing affordability, wage growth, job creation for non-college-educated workers, and entry-level healthcare access are the material concerns of renters and younger adults in a $60K median-income district. The 35% opposition is most likely concentrated here, and Cobb's durability depends on preventing mobilization of this coalition around specific economic grievances; a challenger who builds a renter-and-young-adult coalition around housing affordability or wage policy is the most credible threat to the 65% margin." },
          { name: "Hispanic and Black Community Service in an Older White District", tag: "careful", why: "The combined Hispanic (13.02%) and Black (11.44%) population totals 24.46% of the district—a substantial minority in a 70%-white community where the dominant political narrative focuses on older homeowner concerns. These communities face distinct economic mobility constraints, healthcare equity gaps, and economic opportunity challenges that can become invisible in a district-wide strategy focused on property tax protection and fixed-income security. Authentic engagement with Hispanic and Black constituents—through language access, specific community concern acknowledgment, and economic mobility focus—is both a governance obligation and a latent electoral opportunity to expand beyond the 65% base without alienating homeowner voters." },
          { name: "Treating 65% as a Secure, Expanding Margin", tag: "avoid", why: "A 65% result in a structurally older, homeowner-dominant district is durable but not expanding—demographic change is slow in FL-HD-26, which means the coalition is stable but not growing. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 65% represents a rising baseline or a winnable path to 70% is misreading the district's demographic trajectory. The path to growth, if any, lies in modest expansion among renters and younger adults or improved engagement with Hispanic and Black voters, not in further consolidation of an already-consolidated older homeowner base." },
          { name: "Dismissing Renter Concerns as a Non-Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "A 25.5% renter rate represents roughly one-quarter of the district's households—a population large enough to swing a close election if mobilized effectively around housing affordability or economic opportunity. Any candidate who dismisses renters as temporary residents or non-stakeholders in the community is both factually wrong about the district's composition and strategically vulnerable to a challenger who mobilizes this population around specific grievances. In a district where the opposition is 35%, renters are the most plausible source of alternative coalition-building." }
