          "Lead on property tax protection and fixed-income economic security: at 52.5-year median age with 74.5% homeownership, the constituency is asset-rich but income-constrained, and property tax burden, healthcare cost management, and utility affordability are the direct material concerns driving voting behavior. Any challenger must offer concrete property tax or fixed-income support to threaten Cobb's position.",
          "Attend to the 25.5% renter population and younger adults as a latent opposition coalition: renters and households under 45 are the most likely sources of the 35% opposition, and their concerns around housing affordability, wage growth, and entry-level economic opportunity differ sharply from the older homeowner majority. Cobb consolidates the homeowner base by acknowledging renter concerns without alienating homeowners, or risks a challenger building a renter-and-young-adult coalition that shifts 4-5 points.",
          "Engage the 24.46% combined Hispanic and Black population with specific economic and healthcare attention: these communities exist within a 70%-white, older-skewing district where their needs can become invisible to a legislature focused on the dominant demographic. Language access, healthcare equity, and economic mobility for these communities are constituent needs that deserve direct legislative attention, not assumption into broader messaging.",
          "Recognize that 65% is a durable but not expanding margin: demographic stability (older, homeowner-dominant, slowly changing) means the district is unlikely to swing dramatically, but it is also unlikely to grow more Republican if opposition mobilization improves. Cobb's strategic task is consolidation and modest expansion among the 35%, not assumption that 65% represents a floor."
