          { name: "Aging Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 48.4 median age, 81.4% homeownership, and $66,870 income, FL-HD-27 is a district of older, asset-holding homeowners for whom healthcare costs, property tax management, and long-term care affordability are material daily concerns. State policy on Medicare supplemental coverage, Medicaid for long-term care, and property tax relief directly affects this constituency's ability to age in place. A representative who leads with concrete solutions to these aging-specific economic challenges rather than abstract anti-government messaging demonstrates understanding of who actually lives in the district and what they need." },
          { name: "Understanding the 32% Opposition", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a district this demographically aligned with Republican voters—73% white, older, affluent, homeowner-dominant—a 32% Democratic result cannot be explained by demographic inevitability alone. Whether driven by healthcare cost frustration, property tax fairness concerns, or dissatisfaction with incumbent governance, identifying and specifically addressing what generated that 32% is the single most important thing any candidate can do. A challenger who explicitly campaigns on economic security for aging voters could mobilize that opposition into 40%+ if the incumbent is perceived as indifferent to older constituents' concerns." },
          { name: "Minority Community Visibility & Service", tag: "careful", why: "With 16.3% Hispanic, 6.4% Black, and 1.4% Asian populations, FL-HD-27 has meaningful minority communities whose economic and social concerns can become invisible in a district dominated by older white homeowners. While these voters are not the swing constituency, they represent enough votes to matter in a competitive cycle and deserve direct constituent attention. A legislative agenda that acknowledges minority community needs—economic mobility, healthcare access, fair treatment in government services—rather than assuming they are peripheral demonstrates inclusive governance." },
          { name: "Treating 68% as Immune to Democratic Challenge", tag: "avoid", why: "A 68% Republican result is not a mandate to ignore opposition concerns; it is a plurality in a district where 32% actively chose a Democrat despite overwhelming demographic alignment with Republican voters. Any incumbent who assumes this margin is durable and campaigns as though consolidating only the existing base will be vulnerable to a challenger who specifically addresses older voters' healthcare and economic security concerns with credible policy solutions." },
          { name: "Anti-Tax Absolutism Without Addressing Aging-Specific Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "Generic anti-tax rhetoric disconnected from the material concerns of a 48.4-year-old, property-owning constituency is less credible than messaging that acknowledges real costs—healthcare, long-term care, property tax fairness—and offers specific policy remedies. A representative who responds to aging voters' concerns with 'all taxes are bad' rather than 'here is how state policy protects your ability to age in place' will find that messaging increasingly hollow as healthcare costs continue rising." }
