          { name: "Middle-Class Cost-of-Living Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "FL-HD-30 is a 51.5-year-old, 79% homeowner, $67,825-income suburban constituency with high fixed costs and finite income growth — property taxes, healthcare expenses, and retirement security are the material governance questions this demographic evaluates on. The 34% opposition in a district this affluent suggests that a meaningful portion of the constituency does not believe the incumbent is adequately addressing these cost pressures. A legislative agenda centered on property tax fairness, healthcare affordability, and pension sustainability for older adults directly serves the economic security concerns that drive electoral decisions in this district." },
          { name: "College-Educated Voter Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 29.5% college attainment — above state baseline — HD-30 contains a constituency that evaluates legislative positions through an education and professional-opportunity lens. This group is responsive to messaging on higher education affordability, workforce development, and whether policy reflects genuine engagement with their values around education quality and professional advancement. A candidate who can speak with specificity about education funding, student debt, and the state's role in supporting professional opportunity resonates with a cohort that currently appears underutilized in partisan coalitional messaging." },
          { name: "Understanding the 34% Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "A 34% opposition margin in a district with $67,825 income, 79% homeownership, and significant college attainment is larger than demographic baseline would predict — suggesting concentrated discontent with incumbent positioning on specific issues rather than diffuse ideological opposition. Whether this opposition concentrates among older adults frustrated by healthcare costs, younger renters concerned about housing affordability, or education-focused voters skeptical of incumbent priorities, identifying the composition and driving concerns of that 34% is essential to either consolidating the 66% (if it is genuinely held) or recognizing that a credible challenger can expand that opposition into a winning coalition." },
          { name: "Treating 66% as an Invulnerable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66-34 result in a district this affluent, educated, and cost-conscious is not a commanding mandate — it is a plurality of a constituency that is actively evaluating whether government is delivering on their economic security concerns. In a district where 29.5% college attainment means voters are accustomed to evidence-based policy analysis, and where median age of 51.5 means the constituency has accumulated experience with government performance or failure, any incumbent who campaigns as though 66% is a durable floor rather than an ongoing conversation will find that voter sophistication and cost-of-living pressures can move the margin significantly in a competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Assuming Inherited Partisan Loyalty Over Specific Governance Performance", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated, middle-class suburban voters — particularly older adults managing fixed costs on stable incomes — evaluate electoral choice on whether government is delivering concrete results on the issues that affect their daily lives, not on inherited partisan identity. An incumbent who relies on Republican affiliation rather than demonstrating concrete results on property tax management, healthcare affordability, or economic competitiveness will find that this educated constituency is perfectly capable of voting for an alternative if the alternative offers more credible positioning on the issues they care about." }
