          { name: "Conflating Immigrant and Established Hispanic Communities", tag: "avoid", why: "South Florida's Hispanic population includes both long-established Cuban-American and Puerto Rican communities and more recently arrived Central American and Caribbean immigrant households. These communities have distinct economic stakes, immigration policy preferences, and generational relationships to electoral politics. A representative who addresses 'the Hispanic community' as monolithic misses crucial distinctions: recently arrived immigrant families facing labor-market discrimination and documentation barriers have different policy priorities than second and third-generation Hispanic voters concerned with educational attainment and wealth-building." }
