          "Engage the 43% opposition directly on economic grounds: understand whether it concentrates among Hispanic small-business owners skeptical of regulations, non-Hispanic white homeowners concerned about property rights, or persuadable swing Hispanic voters. Address their specific economic concerns rather than assuming Republican opposition is monolithic or immovable.",
          "Build Hispanic community loyalty through tangible economic delivery, not cultural symbolism: wage growth relative to cost of living, childcare affordability, public education improvement, and healthcare access are the metrics that determine whether Hispanic voters consolidate the 57% or drift toward the 43%. Identity recognition matters, but material improvement in household economics determines durability.",
          "Monitor immigrant and second-generation Hispanic community separately: immigration policy sentiment, language access, and economic opportunity for recently arrived households are distinct from economic concerns of established Hispanic families. A representative who addresses only the established community risks losing persuadable swing voters among newer arrivals or their children."
