          { name: "Hispanic Economic Integration & Small-Business Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 29.01% Hispanic population is the second-largest demographic group and is not voting as a Democratic bloc—their support for or acquiescence to the Republican candidate is the swing factor that explains the 16-point margin in a state where Republicans routinely exceed 65%. The 22.4% college attainment and $58,475 median income signal a working-class community seeking economic mobility through homeownership and entrepreneurship. Any candidate must engage the Hispanic community through Spanish-language outreach focused on workforce development, contractor licensing, small-business access to capital, and the concrete pathways from wage work to economic independence—not through token cultural recognition." },
          { name: "Understanding the Opposition's Composition", tag: "careful", why: "The 42% opposition in a Republican state is mathematically substantial and demands granular understanding of its source. Whether the opposition is concentrated among Black voters (17.15%), renters (28%), less-educated households, or younger adults (median age 43.6 suggests an aging but not elderly constituency), identifying which subgroups produce the 42% and what specific concerns animate their opposition is essential for both defending and challenging this seat. A 58-42 result suggests either a mixed opposition drawn across multiple constituencies, or a strongly mobilized subgroup; determining which changes the strategic response." },
          { name: "Treating the 58% as a Landslide", tag: "avoid", why: "A 16-point margin in a Republican state is solid but not dominant—Florida routinely produces 60-40 or wider GOP results in competitive districts, making 58-42 a baseline Republican performance, not a commanding hold. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns as though 58% represents an insurmountable majority, rather than a working coalition of homeowners and partially integrated Hispanic voters, will be strategically blind to the actual competitive dynamics of the seat." },
          { name: "Identity-Based or Ideological Messaging Without Economic Grounding", tag: "avoid", why: "This district does not reward abstract conservative ideology or cultural messaging disconnected from material household concerns—the 22.4% college attainment, working-class income distribution, and ethnically mixed composition mean voters evaluate candidates on whether government delivers on property taxes, neighborhood safety, healthcare affordability, and economic opportunity, not on partisan identity or cultural positioning. Any candidate who leads with ideology rather than concrete economic governance will fail to motivate or consolidate the diverse working-class constituency that produces the 58-42 result." }
