          { name: "Hispanic Community Demographic Growth and Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 21.06% Hispanic population is substantial and, given the 21.9% renter rate in a 79% homeowner district, is disproportionately concentrated in the renter and younger-adult categories. This is a growing demographic constituency whose economic stakes—housing affordability, education quality, workforce development, and economic opportunity—are distinct from the established homeowner base. The 40% opposition in this district is sufficiently large that engaging the Hispanic community with specific attention to their material concerns (not token acknowledgment) is both a governance obligation and a clear path to understanding and addressing the opposition." },
          { name: "College-Educated Professional Voters and Quality-of-Government Expectations", tag: "careful", why: "The 31.9% college attainment rate signals a professional, managerial constituency that evaluates candidates on competence and results rather than partisan loyalty or ideology. This demographic is both more likely to split tickets and more likely to organize an alternative if they conclude the incumbent is underperforming. In a 60-40 seat with high educational attainment, any portion of the 40% opposition concentrated among college-educated homeowners represents a vulnerability to a credible alternative candidate who can credibly position as a better manager of local governance." },
          { name: "Assuming 60% is a Durable Floor Rather Than a Contingent Consolidation", tag: "avoid", why: "The 60-40 margin is substantial but not overwhelming in a district as economically secure and demographically stable as FL-HD-54. A district where four in five households are homeowners, college attainment is above one-third, and median age is 41.9 is a constituency that does not vote on inherited partisan loyalty—it votes on whether government is delivering competent management. Any campaign that assumes 60% is safe and therefore concentrates on activating the base rather than understanding and addressing the 40% opposition is arithmetically and strategically wrong." },
          { name: "Treating the Hispanic Community as a Monolithic Bloc Rather Than a Diverse Constituency with Distinct Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "At 21.06% of the district and disproportionately concentrated in the renter and younger-adult categories, the Hispanic community has economic and civic concerns—housing affordability, education quality, workforce development, language access to government services—that are specific and material. Any campaign that approaches Hispanic voters primarily through cultural or ethnic framing rather than addressing the concrete economic concerns that shape their daily lives will find that approach ineffective against a challenger who engages these issues with specificity." }
