          { name: "Housing Affordability and Property Tax Management", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 35.3% renters and 64.7% homeowners at a median household income of $49,274, the vast majority of constituents face direct monthly housing cost pressure. In Florida's environment of rising property insurance, property taxes, and rent increases, housing affordability is the material economic concern that shapes voting behavior more durably than cultural positioning. A representative who offers concrete policy responses to housing costs — property tax caps, insurance reform, tenant protections — is addressing the primary economic stress point for the constituency." },
          { name: "Wage-Level Employment and Workforce Development", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 17.9% college-attainment rate is well below state and national averages, indicating a constituency that competes for employment based on high school credentials in an economy increasingly stratified by education. At $49,274 median income, most households are dependent on consistent wage-level work in manufacturing, service, logistics, or trade sectors. Local economic development, apprenticeship programs, and wage standards are material governance concerns for a constituency whose economic security depends on the availability of sustainable high-school-credential employment." },
          { name: "Unaccounted Minority Population and Changing Demographic Composition", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories total 96.6%, leaving a 3.4% gap that in Florida's diverse electoral landscape likely represents additional Hispanic, Caribbean, or other minority residents not captured in the headline 16.21% Hispanic figure. This suggests the actual minority constituency is 5-7 percentage points larger than reported, which is strategically significant because it means the district's true demographic composition is less white-dominant than the 74% headline indicates. Any candidate who campaigns or governs based on the visible demographic numbers is both underestimating the minority constituency and missing a potential electoral vulnerability if turnout or candidate preference among minority voters shifts." },
          { name: "Overinterpreting the 65% Margin as Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "A 65-35 victory in a district with $49,274 median income, high renter concentration, and low college attainment is not a stable majority expressing permanent Republican alignment — it is a current result dependent on continued voter satisfaction with the incumbent's response to housing costs, employment opportunity, and economic security. In an economically stressed constituency, margins shift when a challenger leads credibly on pocketbook concerns or when turnout among renters or minority voters increases relative to the baseline. Treating this seat as safe rather than as a coalition that must be actively maintained is the primary risk to holding it." },
          { name: "Cultural Messaging Substituting for Economic Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency with 35.3% renters at $49,274 income and 17.9% college attainment is not primarily motivated by abstract cultural or social conservatism — it is motivated by whether it can pay rent, keep a home, and find stable work. A representative who leads on cultural issues while offering vague or absent responses to housing costs, employment opportunity, and wage standards is misaligning campaign messaging with constituent economic reality. In a district this economically constrained, specificity on pocketbook concerns is not optional positioning — it is the prerequisite for durable electoral support." }
