          { name: "Homeowner Economic Security & Property Rights Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "FL-HD-79 is 77.8% homeowner with $74,987 median income and 49.4 median age—a constituency whose primary economic asset is residential property and whose primary economic anxiety is preservation of that asset's value and affordability of ownership costs. Property tax management, school funding that drives neighborhood desirability, and infrastructure investment in residential areas are the material governance issues that hold this coalition. A Republican representative who delivers on these specific, local property-owner concerns—rather than abstract national ideology—reinforces the reason this constituency votes Republican at 69%." },
          { name: "School Quality & Education Investment as Affluent-Parent Legitimacy Metric", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 25.8% college-educated rate (higher than state average) combined with 49.4 median age and high homeownership suggests a substantial population of educated parents whose residential location choice is partially driven by school district quality. Education funding, teacher compensation, school infrastructure, and curriculum quality are the direct governance issues on which these voters evaluate representation. Any Democratic challenger who campaigns on education investment has a credible opening to this affluent, educated constituency if the Republican incumbent is not visibly delivering on school quality; this is the single most persuadable issue for the 69% coalition." },
          { name: "Renter Minority Economic Concerns & Affordability Messaging", tag: "careful", why: "The 22.2% renter rate represents 22,000-25,000 residents with fundamentally different economic incentives than the homeowner majority—facing housing costs, service-sector wages, and cost-of-living pressures that the Republican base does not experience. At 69-31, the renter minority is currently politically inactive or aligned with the incumbent, but they represent the most likely source of electoral volatility in a more competitive cycle or if Democratic messaging specifically targets housing affordability and economic mobility. Ignoring this constituency invites concentrated Democratic organizing in future cycles; engaging them directly (even if not winning them) prevents opposition from consolidating a counter-base." },
          { name: "Treating 69-31 as a Safe Floor Rather Than Coalition-Dependent Ceiling", tag: "avoid", why: "A 69-31 result is strong, but it reflects the current strength of the homeowner-affluent-educated coalition, not a durable floor that survives regardless of performance or challenger profile. In a district at this income level with significant college education, a well-resourced Democratic challenger with specific messaging on education investment, property tax fairness, or service-sector economic mobility could shift the margin toward 62-38 or 60-40 within a cycle. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 69% is unshakeable rather than something that must be actively maintained is committing a strategic error." },
          { name: "Engaging the Demographic Gap as 'Other' Rather Than Specific Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "The ~3.7% gap in the four reported demographic categories may seem small, but it represents roughly 3,700-4,500 residents whose political preferences and governance concerns are not visible in the partisan margin. These may be recent immigrants, multiracial households, or other communities with specific interests around immigration policy, civil rights, or economic inclusion. Passive invisibility of this population invites opponent organizers to define what these voters care about; proactive engagement (even brief or limited) prevents concentration of opposition messaging and maintains the possibility of holding at least 50-60% of this constituency." }
