          { name: "Property Tax Administration and Fixed-Income Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "An 81.96% white, 80.8% homeowner constituency with $93,456 income and 63.1-year median age is acutely sensitive to property tax burden, homestead exemption adequacy, and the cumulative cost-of-living pressures that erode wealth in retirement. Rising insurance costs, property tax increases, and community service fees create real financial stress for homeowners on fixed incomes, even at above-average income levels. Candidates who offer specific tax administration solutions — not just calls for across-the-board cuts that undermine community services — can credibly position themselves as protecting this constituency's material interests." },
          { name: "Demographic Aging and Generational Replacement Risk", tag: "careful", why: "The 63.1-year median age is substantially older than Florida's state median and creates a structural vulnerability if in-migration or demographic replacement accelerates: younger cohorts entering the district may not share the same Republican alignment as the aging base. A 69-31 margin held by a constituency aging faster than it is being replaced is mathematically stable in the near term but fragile over a 10-15 year horizon. Any candidate who ignores this risk or campaigns as though the current margin reflects durable structural advantage rather than demographic dependency is strategically underestimating the competitive environment." },
          { name: "Ideological Rigidity on Pragmatic Governance Issues", tag: "avoid", why: "The 46.2% college-educated constituency is substantially more responsive to policy substance and governing competence than to partisan positioning. Candidates who take ideologically fixed positions on issues where this educated, affluent constituency expects pragmatic problem-solving — infrastructure maintenance, community service delivery, healthcare administration — risk being outflanked by opponents who position themselves as more competent administrators. This constituency will punish perceived rigidity more severely than less-educated electorates." },
          { name: "Taking the Base for Granted Without Addressing Emerging Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "A 69-31 margin can create false confidence that the base is locked in and does not require active engagement on their actual concerns. Aging homeowners whose primary focus is healthcare sustainability, property tax fairness, and infrastructure maintenance can become genuinely alienated if they perceive the incumbent as taking their vote for granted while pursuing an ideological agenda disconnected from their material interests. Any challenger who credibly demonstrates superior competence on these core issues has an opening, even in a heavily Republican district, if the incumbent is perceived as complacent." }
