          { name: "Renter Constituency and Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "At 22.8% renter rate in a district with $53,480 median income, FL-HD-83 has a meaningful renter constituency living in a tight housing market where rental costs consume a significant share of household income. The 25% opposition may include renters facing cost-of-living pressure who evaluate representation based on housing affordability, tenant protections, and whether government is delivering economic security. In a 75-25 district, the renter vote is not large enough to determine the outcome alone, but mobilizing renters around anti-incumbent messaging — or securing their support through specific housing policy — can shift a 75-25 margin toward a more vulnerable 70-30 or 68-32 if challenger campaigns effectively to this group." },
          { name: "Dismissing the 25% Opposition as Locked-In Democratic Votes", tag: "avoid", why: "A 75% result in a constituency with one-quarter Hispanic population, 22.8% renters, and $53,480 income should not be interpreted as a mathematically stable coalition. The opposition is not necessarily locked in — it may represent persuadable voters who chose the opposition candidate based on specific policy failures or missed constituent engagement. Any incumbent who treats the 25% as a fixed Democratic base rather than as persuadable constituents with real economic concerns (housing costs, healthcare access, workforce opportunity) risks a credible challenger narrowing the margin by 10+ points through targeted engagement of renters, Hispanic voters, and lower-income households." },
          { name: "Treating Hispanic Voters as a Monolithic Democratic Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "Hispanic voters at 25.51% of the district are not inherently Democratic — the 75-25 result itself indicates substantial Hispanic support for the Republican incumbent or weak Democratic turnout among Hispanic voters. Any campaign strategy that assumes Hispanic voters will automatically support a Democratic challenger, or that treats Hispanic engagement as secondary to Anglo voter consolidation, misreads both the current electoral reality and the persuadable nature of a constituency at this income and housing-cost profile. Hispanic households evaluating representation based on economic mobility, business opportunity, and family economic security are capable of supporting either party if the candidate demonstrates genuine constituent attention." }
