        issues: [
          { name: "College-Educated, High-Income Constituency Consolidation", tag: "lean-into", why: "FL-HD-91 is the most educated and affluent district in this analysis cohort: 56% college-educated adults and $88,077 median household income represent a constituency that evaluates candidates on competence, institutional credibility, and professional record rather than partisan rhetoric. The 55-45 Republican margin in a district with these demographics signals that college-educated voters are genuinely contested and responsive to candidate quality and specific policy competence. The Republican incumbent must lead on demonstrating executive capacity and substantive governance expertise to consolidate this educated majority and prevent defection to a credible Democratic challenger." },
          { name: "Aging Homeowner Base & Retirement Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "With a 49.3-year median age, 69% homeownership implicit in the 31% renter rate, and $88,077 income concentrated among asset-holding households, FL-HD-91 is fundamentally a district of middle-aged and older homeowners in or approaching retirement. Healthcare costs, property tax management, and long-term care access are the direct material concerns of this constituency, particularly for households managing fixed incomes on investment returns. The Republican incumbent should lead with concrete solutions on healthcare affordability and retirement security that resonate with voters who have spent decades building home equity and now face rising healthcare costs that threaten it." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Engagement & Economic Mobility", tag: "careful", why: "The 16.52% Hispanic population represents the second-largest demographic community in FL-HD-91, yet the district's political narrative is dominated by the college-educated, high-income white majority (68.8%). Hispanic households in this district have material concerns around economic mobility, housing affordability, and healthcare access that are distinct from the retirement security focus of older homeowners. At 16.52%, the Hispanic community is large enough that differential turnout or candidate preference shifts could materially affect a 55-45 margin, particularly if Democratic outreach identifies Hispanic economic concerns that the Republican incumbent has treated as secondary to the aging homeowner base." },
          { name: "Partisan Polarization as Governance Model", tag: "avoid", why: "FL-HD-91's 55-45 margin in a district with 56% college-educated voters means the constituency has genuine capacity to evaluate candidates on post-partisan governance competence and institutional problem-solving. Any candidate who defaults to partisan base-mobilization framing or treats the 45% opposition as ideologically opposed rather than genuinely contested constituents will forgo the opportunity to consolidate the college-educated, high-income voters who are the actual decision-makers in this district. The educated, affluent constituency in FL-HD-91 evaluates on whether their representative solves problems, not whether they perform partisan loyalty." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Democratic 45% as Structurally Unwinnable", tag: "avoid", why: "A 55-45 result in a district this educated and affluent is a genuinely competitive race, not a durable Republican hold. In a constituency where 56% hold college degrees and $88,077 is the median household income, the 45% Democratic opposition is not ideologically alien — it is college-educated, affluent voters evaluating on competence and specific policy outcomes. Any Republican incumbent who campaigns as though the 45% is fixed opposition rather than contested voters responding to governance performance and concrete policy delivery will find that assumption tested in the next competitive cycle when a credible Democratic challenger emerges with specific solutions to the healthcare and retirement security concerns that currently divide the district." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "FL-HD-91: A College-Educated, High-Income District Where 45% Opposition Signals the Seat Is Genuinely Contested, Not Safely Republican.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "FL-HD-91 is a fundamentally affluent, educated district that defies the partisan simplicity of a 55-45 Republican margin. With 56% college-educated adults, $88,077 median household income, and 49.3 median age concentrated among homeowner households, this is a constituency of professionals, business owners, and retirees who evaluate candidates primarily on competence, institutional credibility, and specific policy solutions rather than partisan tribal affiliation. The 45% Democratic vote share in a district this educated and affluent is the most important analytical signal: it indicates that nearly half the district's college-educated, high-income voters actively chose the Democratic candidate, suggesting that the Republican incumbent's hold is conditional on continued performance on issues that matter to affluent homeowners—primarily healthcare costs, property tax fairness, and retirement security. In the context of Florida's broader political dynamics, a 55-45 margin in a high-income, educated district is not a comfortable floor; it is a genuinely competitive result that depends on the incumbent's capacity to demonstrate superior governance competence and deliver specific solutions to the economic concerns that drive affluent voters' electoral decisions.",
          "The 16.52% Hispanic population represents a second demographic layer that current partisan analysis may underweight. Hispanic households in FL-HD-91, concentrated in what is likely a professional and service-sector workforce, have distinct economic concerns—housing affordability, education quality, and economic mobility for their children—that diverge from the retirement security focus of the aging white homeowner majority. Turnout and candidate preference among Hispanic voters in a district this close could materially affect the outcome, particularly if Democratic outreach identifies specific economic solutions to housing costs or education investment that the Republican incumbent has treated as secondary to the college-educated, retiree-focused agenda. The Republican incumbent's electoral durability depends not only on consolidating the educated white homeowner base but on demonstrating that solutions to healthcare and economic opportunity extend authentically to the Hispanic community as well—not as an afterthought, but as part of a unified economic security agenda that addresses the district's full demographic composition."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with healthcare affordability and retirement security solutions: at 49.3 median age with $88,077 income concentrated among homeowners, the constituency's primary economic vulnerability is rising healthcare costs threatening accumulated home equity and fixed retirement income—specific policy proposals on Medicare negotiation and long-term care access matter more than partisan framing.",
          "Consolidate college-educated voters on governance competence: 56% college attainment means the 45% opposition is not ideologically alien but competing on institutional credibility and problem-solving capacity—demonstrate that record through specific legislative achievements and policy expertise, not partisan loyalty.",
          "Engage the 16.52% Hispanic community on housing affordability and education investment as part of the same economic security agenda: this community is large enough to shift the margin in a 55-45 district, and current focus on aging homeowners may be leaving Hispanic economic concerns under-addressed and vulnerable to Democratic outreach.",
          "Treat 55-45 as competitive, not safe: with nearly half the college-educated, affluent constituency actively voting Democratic, the incumbent's margin is conditional on continued performance and specific delivery on healthcare and economic concerns—any complacency about the seat's stability invites a credible Democratic challenger to exploit governance gaps."
        ]
