          { name: "Renter Economic Security and Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 40.2% of the district, renters represent a substantial constituency whose economic concerns — housing costs, tenant protections, and residential stability — are materially different from homeowners' and often underaddressed in affluent suburban politics. In a 57-43 seat, losing even modest renter support to a challenger who centers housing affordability and tenant rights is sufficient to flip the margin. The incumbent must demonstrate concrete policy engagement with renter concerns, not assume they will remain loyal to a homeowner-oriented Republican agenda." },
          { name: "College-Educated Voter Persuadability on Democracy and Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 47.3% college attainment rate in an affluent suburb has become a volatile constituency: educated voters, particularly homeowners in this income range, have demonstrated increasing willingness to ticket-split or abandon party loyalty over concerns about democratic institutions, rule of law, and competent governance. The incumbent's 57% margin depends on consolidating educated homeowners on economic management and governance competence, not assuming education automatically produces Republican loyalty. Failing to demonstrate serious engagement with governance quality and institutional integrity creates space for opposition messaging to persuade college-educated swing voters." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Engagement and Economic Mobility", tag: "careful", why: "At 19.48% of the district, the Hispanic population in an affluent, educated suburban context is persuadable based on economic opportunity, healthcare access, and perceived candidate respect — not monolithic partisan loyalty. The incumbent's engagement with this community must extend beyond token acknowledgment to demonstrate concrete policy and constituent service attention to economic mobility, small business support, and healthcare access concerns. A challenger who invests in direct Hispanic community organizing can credibly compete for this bloc even in a 57% Republican seat if the incumbent treats them as peripheral to the core strategy." },
          { name: "Dismissing the 43% Opposition as Unpersuadable", tag: "avoid", why: "The 43% opposition in a college-educated, affluent suburb is not a fixed Democratic base but rather a persuadable coalition of educated swing voters, renters with economic concerns, and minority community members evaluating the incumbent's governance record. Any candidate who treats this 43% as ideologically opposed rather than genuinely persuadable — and thus fails to address their material concerns — is abandoning the swing voters who hold the seat's electoral balance. In a 57-43 district, the opposition is not a permanent constituency to be ignored but a winning coalition waiting for a candidate who speaks to their actual concerns." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Safely Republican Based on Income and Education", tag: "avoid", why: "Affluent, educated suburban districts nationally have become less reliably Republican, particularly when the constituency includes a substantial renter population (40%), minority communities (28%), and educated voters concerned about institutional governance. The incumbent's 57% margin is not a reflection of inherent Republican advantage in this demographic profile but rather a specific alignment between the incumbent's appeal and the current voter coalition — an alignment that is vulnerable to erosion if a challenger invests in renter outreach, Hispanic community organizing, or educated voter persuasion on governance competence. Assuming the 57% will hold without active engagement across the entire district misreads the competitive reality of affluent, educated suburban constituencies in contemporary Florida." }
