        issues: [
          { name: "Education Funding & K-12 Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 44.4% college-educated adults in FL-SD-10, this is a constituency that evaluates school quality and education investment as a primary governance metric. In a suburban district with median household income at $83,982 and a 36% renter rate, property-owning families and young professionals with children (or contemplating them) are directly evaluating whether public education meets their expectations. Specific commitments on teacher compensation, curriculum rigor, special education access, and school infrastructure are the concrete deliverables that college-educated suburban voters use to distinguish between candidates." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access & Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a district with median income at $83,982 and median age at 39.7, healthcare cost management and access are material concerns for middle-aged professionals and families navigating insurance options, prescription costs, and preventive care. The 22.4% Hispanic/Latino community includes both insured professionals and working families for whom healthcare affordability is a real constraint. Specific positions on Medicaid expansion, insurance marketplace stability, prescription drug pricing, and mental health access are the policy-level commitments that distinguish a candidate who understands this constituency's material concerns from one offering generic healthcare rhetoric." },
          { name: "Hispanic/Latino Community Engagement Across Class Lines", tag: "careful", why: "At 22.4%, Hispanic/Latino residents are the largest minority group in FL-SD-10 and include both suburban business owners and professionals evaluating on economic policy and a working and service-sector population evaluating on wages, benefits, and economic mobility. Any candidate who treats this 22.4% as a monolithic voting bloc or relies on cultural messaging rather than specific economic policy alignment will miss the opportunity to build durable support among the professionals and business owners in this community. The college-educated, high-income profile of the district means that Hispanic/Latino voters here have material stakes in tax policy, regulation, and business environment — issues where specificity and economic substance matter more than cultural positioning." },
          { name: "Ideological Overreach on Cultural Issues", tag: "avoid", why: "A 44.4% college-educated suburban constituency at 39.7 median age is precisely the demographic that punishes perceived ideological extremism or cultural overreach — particularly on education curriculum, LGBTQ+ issues, and reproductive rights, where college-educated professionals tend to hold moderate-to-libertarian views emphasizing local control and parental choice. Any candidate who leads with cultural conservatism or positions that align with partisan extremes risks driving college-educated, moderate suburban voters toward the 46% opposition and eroding the professional credibility that holds the 54% coalition together." },
          { name: "Taking the 54% for Granted as a Durable Base", tag: "avoid", why: "In a high-income, college-educated suburban district, the 54% result reflects conditional support from professionals whose votes are available to a credible alternative if the incumbent is perceived as delivering poor results, ideological excess, or incompetent governance. Any candidate who treats 54% as a permissive margin and campaigns defensively rather than demonstrating measurable improvements on education, healthcare, and economic opportunity is systematically underestimating the volatility of college-educated suburban voters when faced with a credible alternative." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "FL-SD-10: A 54-46 Republican Hold in a High-Income, College-Educated District Where Suburban Professional Voters Demand Competence and Specificity, Not Ideology.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "FL-SD-10 is a solidly Republican district at 54-46, but the underlying constituency profile reveals why that margin is not as durable as the partisan result might suggest. With a median household income of $83,982, college-educated adults at 44.4%, and a median age of 39.7, this is a suburban professional constituency — homeowners, business owners, and white-collar workers whose votes are contingent on perception of competent governance, not inherited party loyalty. The 36% renter rate signals a non-trivial population of younger, more mobile professionals and families who evaluate candidates on performance and specific policy alignment rather than straight-ticket partisanship. The demographic diversity — 57.3% white, 22.4% Hispanic/Latino, 11.7% Black, 5.1% Asian — is substantially more diverse than Oklahoma's state average and reflects the kind of suburban heterogeneity where coalition-building across racial and ethnic lines is not optional but essential to holding a seat. Brodeur's 54% win in 2024 is a legitimate Republican hold, but it is not a commanding margin in a district whose income, education, and diversity profile suggests voters who will punish perceived incompetence or ideological overreach.",
          "The strategic vulnerability in FL-SD-10 lies in the tension between the 54% Republican result and the 44.4% college-educated, $83,982-income profile that typically produces more competitive margins in suburban districts nationally. A 46% Democratic opposition in a high-income, college-educated suburban constituency is the kind of signal that demands specific explanation: whether it reflects suburban professional concern about education funding and public school quality, healthcare access and affordability, housing cost management, or specific governance failures by the incumbent is the critical diagnostic question. The Hispanic/Latino community at 22.4% is the largest minority group in the district and cannot be taken for granted in a district this education- and income-heavy; suburban Hispanic professionals and business owners evaluate on economic policy and competent administration, not cultural messaging. Any challenger must understand specifically what generated the 46% — and any incumbent must recognize that 54% in this demographic profile is not a permissive margin but a warning that continued success depends on delivering tangible results on the issues that matter most to college-educated, middle-to-upper-income suburban professionals: education quality, healthcare sustainability, housing affordability, and efficient governance."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Diagnose the 46% opposition with specificity: identify whether it concentrates among suburban professionals frustrated by education funding, younger renters concerned about housing costs, Hispanic/Latino small business owners evaluating on economic policy, or some combination — and offer concrete answers rather than partisan boilerplate.",
          "Lead with competent, results-oriented governance on the issues that matter to college-educated, high-income suburban voters: K-12 education quality and funding, property tax fairness, healthcare access and affordability, and infrastructure efficiency — not cultural or ideological positioning that alienates college-educated professionals across demographic lines.",
          "Build active relationships with the 22.4% Hispanic/Latino community as suburban business owners and professionals, not as a monolithic voting bloc; this constituency evaluates on economic policy specificity, education investment, and whether government is delivering for them — not cultural appeals that feel performative to voters with real stakes in local prosperity.",
          "Recognize that 54% in a $83,982-income, 44.4% college-educated suburban district is a working majority, not a safe seat; continued success depends on demonstrating that the incumbent (or new candidate) is delivering measurable results on education, healthcare, and economic opportunity — not on expanding the base through ideological appeals that further alienate the college-educated professionals who determine the seat's competitive margin."
        ]
