          { name: "Treating 57% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 57-43 result in a district this educated and economically diverse is not a safe margin; it reflects Republican consolidation of homeowners and older voters while ceding 43% of the electorate to Democratic opposition. The 34.4% college attainment, 30.3% renter rate, and 48.7 median age together create a constituency that evaluates on performance, professional-class economic interests, and whether government is delivering outcomes that improve their quality of life—not inherited partisan loyalty. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though 57% is a secure floor risks underestimating the opposition's capacity to mobilize around specific constituent concerns." },
          { name: "Dismissing Renter & Younger-Adult Constituency Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "Framing housing affordability, healthcare access, education funding, or wage stagnation as special interests or fringe concerns will alienate the 30.3% renter constituency and younger professionals whose economic concerns are real and present. In an affluent, educated district, renters and younger workers are not a marginal constituency—they represent nearly one-third of the district—and their concerns about economic mobility and quality of life are legitimate governance priorities that deserve specific policy responses rather than dismissal as partisan rhetoric." }
