        issues: [
          { name: "Homeowner Economic Security & Property Tax Fairness", tag: "lean-into", why: "FL-SD-22's constituency is 76.2% homeowners with a median age of 56.3 and median income of $80,660 — older, asset-holding households in a high-cost Florida market where property taxes, insurance, and service costs are rising faster than fixed incomes. Property tax relief, homeowner cost control, and the sustainability of services that older homeowners depend on are the material governance concerns for the district's economic majority. Any candidate who leads on these concrete, pocketbook issues rather than abstract economic conservatism is more likely to consolidate and expand the 67% Republican base." },
          { name: "Renter & Younger Adult Cost-of-Living Pressure", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 23.8% renter rate represents approximately one-quarter of the district's households, and in a $80,660-income district, renters are not low-income families but rather younger professionals, service workers, and families priced out of home ownership by Florida's housing market. The 33% Democratic opposition is likely concentrated in part among this renter constituency and younger adults within the district's 56.3-year median age. A candidate who engages this quarter of the district on housing affordability, wage-income alignment with local costs, and healthcare access — rather than dismissing them as inevitable opposition — can persuade some to reconsider and dramatically limits the opposition's ability to mobilize them." },
          { name: "College-Educated Swing Voters & Non-Economic Issue Evaluation", tag: "careful", why: "At 39.6% college attainment in Florida — a swing state where college-educated voters have become a genuinely competitive demographic — a meaningful subset of the district's most economically secure residents are evaluating on issues beyond traditional Republican economics: healthcare access, environmental stewardship, governance tone, and institutional trust. The 33% Democratic share in a 80K-income, college-educated district is not aberrational; it reflects real political evaluation among educated voters who are not reflexively Republican. Any candidate must engage this group on their actual concerns rather than assuming educational attainment reinforces partisanship." },
          { name: "Treating 67% as a Structural Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 67-33 result in a high-income, college-educated district should produce a more comfortable Republican margin than it does; the gap between expected outcome (potentially 70-75% in this demographic profile) and actual result (67%) suggests that structural assumptions about this district are weaker than they appear. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns as though 67% represents an unshakeable coalition rather than a coalition that requires active engagement of homeowners on their cost concerns and persuasion of educated voters on non-economic issues will find the margin eroding in a subsequent cycle." },
          { name: "Dismissing Renter & Younger Adult Constituencies as Unpersuadable", tag: "avoid", why: "The 23.8% renter rate in FL-SD-22 is not a low-income service-sector base but rather younger professionals and families constrained by housing costs in a high-income market — a constituency with genuine economic interests in cost-of-living governance and often persuadable to vote on pocketbook issues if a candidate engages them seriously. Any candidate who writes off this quarter of the district as ideologically Democratic or assumes they cannot be moved abandons 20-25 percentage points of potential persuasion and concedes the opposition an easy mobilization target." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "FL-SD-22: A Wealthy, Aging, College-Educated Republican Stronghold Where a 67-33 Margin Masks Vulnerability Among Renters and Younger Adults in a High-Cost District.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "FL-SD-22 is a heavily Republican district with a 67-33 partisan result that, on surface examination, appears to rest on stable structural advantages: median household income of $80,660 (well above state and national medians), college attainment of 39.6%, and a median age of 56.3 years — the profile of an established, asset-holding, educationally credentialed constituency. The 78.9% white majority and relatively low renter rate of 23.8% further suggest a homeowner-dominant coalition aligned with traditional Republican economics and cultural conservatism. However, the 67-33 margin in what should be a safely Republican district given these demographics warrants scrutiny: in a district this wealthy and educated, with this age profile, a 67-point Republican margin is not extraordinary — but it is also not invincible. The 33% Democratic share in a high-income, college-educated, older district suggests that education and income are not straightforward Republican levers in FL-SD-22 the way they might be in lower-income rural areas, and that a meaningful subset of this district's most economically secure residents are evaluating on non-traditional Republican grounds.",
          "The district's economic profile creates specific vulnerabilities that a challenger must understand and a defender must proactively address. The $80,660 median income places FL-SD-22 squarely in the upper-middle-class range, but in the context of Florida's coastal and near-coastal districts, this income increasingly does not translate to housing affordability, property tax stability, or the retirement security that older residents (median age 56.3) are acutely concerned about. The 23.8% renter rate — while lower than state and national averages — represents approximately one-quarter of the district's households operating in a rental market where housing cost pressure is acute, and renters in high-income districts are often younger professionals, service workers, and families pushed into rental status by home prices rather than income constraints. For a 67-33 seat to shift, the incumbent must actively consolidate the homeowner, college-educated, older majority while a challenger must specifically engage the 23.8% renter constituency, younger adults within the 56.3-year median age district, and college-educated voters evaluating on issues beyond traditional Republican economics — healthcare access, environmental stewardship, or moderate governance tone. The 39.6% college attainment rate is substantial, but in Florida's most competitive districts, college-educated voters have become a swing demographic rather than a Republican base."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Understand the 33% Democratic share as the primary strategic fact: in a $80,660-income, 39.6% college-educated, 56.3-year-old district, a one-third opposition is not trivial, and any candidate must identify specifically whether it concentrates among renters, younger adults, college-educated professionals evaluating on non-economic issues, or some combination — and offer concrete answers rather than assuming demographic alignment holds.",
          "Lead with housing affordability and property tax fairness for the homeowner majority: at $80,660 median income in a high-cost Florida market, older homeowners are facing rising property taxes, insurance costs, and the gap between fixed incomes and rising service costs; concrete policy on property tax relief and homeowner cost control is more likely to consolidate the Republican base than abstract economic messaging.",
          "Engage the 23.8% renter constituency and younger adults with specificity on cost-of-living pressures: renters and younger professionals in FL-SD-22 are not concentrated in low-income service roles but rather in housing-cost-constrained professions; a candidate who dismisses or ignores this quarter of the district concedes it entirely to opposition mobilization.",
          "Recognize that 39.6% college attainment in a swing state means college-educated voters are evaluating on multiple dimensions beyond traditional Republican economics — acknowledge this reality rather than default to messaging that assumes education reinforces partisanship."
        ]
