        issues: [
          { name: "Hispanic Voter Economic Pragmatism & Cost-of-Living Pressures", tag: "lean-into", why: "Hispanic voters at 24.47% of the district are the swing constituency in a 55-45 seat; at $77,526 median household income with substantial homeownership, they are evaluating candidates on concrete economic performance—property tax fairness, insurance affordability, utility costs, childcare access—not inherited partisan loyalty. Polsky's 2024 win depended on holding this community, and any challenger will attempt to persuade them by arguing economic mismanagement. The winning strategy requires specific, credible policy commitments addressing the cost pressures that suburban Miami-Dade Hispanic households actually face, backed by legislative record or concrete implementation plans." },
          { name: "College-Educated Suburban Voter Service Delivery & Quality of Life", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 39.1% college attainment rate in a district with $77,526 income describes a professional and semi-professional constituency concentrated in suburban Miami-Dade, where homeowners and renters evaluate government on competent service delivery, education quality, infrastructure, and whether public institutions function effectively. This is a constituency that will abandon either party if they believe government is failing on basics; Polsky must demonstrate tangible wins on education funding, transportation, and suburban infrastructure to consolidate this vote, while any challenger must argue persuasively that Polsky's legislative record has failed to deliver." },
          { name: "Black Community Constituent Attention in a Diverse District", tag: "careful", why: "At 21.21% of the district, the Black community represents one-fifth of FL-SD-30's population, but in a district where the dominant political story is Hispanic persuadability and white suburban swing voters, Black constituents can receive insufficient attention from both parties. Economic opportunity, education quality, and public safety are the material concerns for this constituency; any candidate who takes Black voter support for granted while focusing on Hispanic and white persuasion risks losing engagement and turnout from a constituency that could shift the outcome in a 55-45 seat if mobilized differently." },
          { name: "Partisan Ideology Framing as a Substitute for Material Governance", tag: "avoid", why: "FL-SD-30's diversity, stable middle-class income, and 55-45 margin reflect a constituency that evaluates candidates on whether government delivers concrete services and economic competence, not on abstract ideological positioning. Any Democrat campaigning primarily on cultural or identity-based appeals without demonstrating economic competence or cost-of-living solutions will fail to persuade the Hispanic and white professional voters who determine the outcome; any Republican campaigning primarily on cultural grievance without addressing the economic pragmatism of suburban homeowners will similarly fail. The winning argument in this district is always about delivery." },
          { name: "Treating a 55-45 Margin as a Durable Democratic Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "Polsky's 55% represents a genuine but narrow win in a district where one-quarter of the population is Hispanic, one-fifth is Black, and two-fifths are college-educated professionals—all constituencies with demonstrated capacity to swing based on performance evaluation rather than inherited party loyalty. Any Democratic incumbent who campaigns as though this seat is safely held, or who neglects the material economic concerns of Hispanic homeowners and renters while focusing on base mobilization, will find that 55% is not a floor but a ceiling that can shift downward if the opposition successfully argues economic mismanagement or service failure. The seat is durable only if the incumbent actively serves it." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Miami-Dade's Diverse Middle Class: A 55-45 Democratic Hold Built on Hispanic Economic Pragmatism, College-Educated Professionals, and Suburban Stability Vulnerable to Anti-Incumbent Messaging.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "FL-SD-30 is a suburban Miami-Dade district defined by demographic diversity, relative economic stability, and genuine political competitiveness despite Polsky's 55% win in 2024. The district's median household income of $77,526 is healthy and the 39.1% college attainment rate signals a professional and semi-professional constituency concentrated in suburban communities where home equity and stable employment matter. The 34.8% renter rate and 41.3 median age together describe a mixed-tenure, middle-aged constituency: homeowners with mortgages and accumulated equity, renters in more precarious economic positions, and a population old enough to have established economic stakes but not so old as to be primarily fixed-income retirees. The 55-45 Democratic margin is genuine but not dominant; it reflects a constituency that split nearly evenly between two parties and is actively evaluating its interests rather than voting from inherited partisan loyalty. Polsky won this seat persuadably, not by consolidating a Democratic base.",
          "The Hispanic population at 24.47%—roughly one-quarter of the district—is the swing constituency that determines whether FL-SD-30 remains Democratic or flips. The 43.33% white and 21.21% Black populations, combined with the Hispanic community, create a genuinely diverse district where no single ethnic group commands a majority. In suburban Miami-Dade, Hispanic voters at this income level ($77,526 median) and education level (39.1% college-educated) are evaluating candidates on economic competence, public safety, education quality, and whether government delivers concrete services—not on abstract cultural positioning. The 2024 result suggests Hispanic voters in FL-SD-30 valued Polsky's profile enough to support her, but a 45% Republican share indicates substantial Republican persuadability within this community. Any Republican challenger who can credibly argue economic mismanagement, rising costs of living, or service delivery failures will find receptive ears among Hispanic homeowners and renters facing real cost pressures. Any Democrat defending this seat must make the affirmative case that their economic record serves this constituency's material interests, not rely on presumed Democratic loyalty among Hispanic voters."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Consolidate the college-educated professional vote (39.1%)—this constituency values competent governance, education investment, and infrastructure—by making specific, credible arguments about local service delivery, school quality, and suburban quality-of-life improvements; vague Democratic messaging will not hold educated suburban voters against a Republican making concrete economic arguments.",
          "Engage Hispanic homeowners and renters as the genuine swing constituency by addressing cost-of-living pressures directly—property taxes, insurance costs, utility rates, childcare affordability—and demonstrating tangible legislative wins on these issues; Hispanic voters in this income range are pragmatists evaluating whether government works for them, not ideological Democrats.",
          "Build durability with the 21.21% Black community through specific constituent attention to economic opportunity, education access, and public safety; in a diverse district where Hispanic and white populations receive disproportionate attention, the Black constituency can become invisible despite representing one-fifth of the district.",
          "Treat the 45% opposition as indicative of a genuinely competitive seat rather than a narrow Democratic win; any incumbent or challenger who campaigns as though one party has a stable base in FL-SD-30 is misreading the district—the margin reflects active persuadability and can shift quickly if material economic conditions or constituent service failures become salient."
        ]
